WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:04.520
A lot of what we see on the big screen is
created with computers and green screens,

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:09.140
but some scenes are still filmed in real places
— and occasionally, once the movie is done

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:12.570
filming, those sets are just left behind to
be reclaimed by nature.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:16.309
Here are some eerie abandoned movie sets you
didn't know still exist.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:17.580
Big Fish

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:22.200
Daniel Wallace's novel Big Fish is set in
the fictional Alabama town of Spectre.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:27.180
When Tim Burton adapted the movie for the
screen in 2003, crews built a real Spectre,

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.869
complete with a church, storefronts, and houses.

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:33.820
And it's all still there, minus a few buildings
that are collapsing or have burned down.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:37.960
Fans of Big Fish can still visit Spectre,
which is housed on a private island outside

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.089
of Montgomery, Alabama, by paying a $3 toll.

00:00:41.089 --> 00:00:44.550
That's one big fish for a pretty small fee...babum
chshh.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:45.889
The Fugitive

00:00:45.889 --> 00:00:50.609
A standout scene from this 1993 Oscar-nominated
Harrison Ford thriller takes place when a

00:00:50.609 --> 00:00:52.530
prison bus collides with a train.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:56.739
Richard Kimble escapes the wreckage to become...well,
you know..

00:00:56.739 --> 00:00:58.369
"The Fugitive"

00:00:58.369 --> 00:01:03.289
Anyway, that crash wasn't CGI — the crew
really did slam a train on a disused portion

00:01:03.289 --> 00:01:05.979
of the Great Smoky Mountain Railroad in North
Carolina.

00:01:05.979 --> 00:01:10.759
It cost $1.5 million to stage, and they only
had one take to get it right.

00:01:10.759 --> 00:01:15.040
Rather than clean it up, The Fugitive crew
just left the train and bus debris behind,

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.430
where it's rusted over time.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.350
It's quite visible on many train excursions
in the area.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:22.969
Not only that, it's probably the only place
you should have this conversation:

00:01:22.969 --> 00:01:26.069
"I didn't kill my wife!"

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:28.979
"I don't care!"

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:29.979
Star Wars

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:34.890
Some of the most recognizable set pieces from
the first Star Wars movies still stand, 40

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:36.710
years after filming started.

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:41.140
Standing in for Luke Skywalker's desert home
planet of Tatooine were the deserts of Tunisia,

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:45.880
and on that terrain sit many instantly recognizable
Star Wars buildings, including Luke Skywalker's

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:50.899
house, parts of Mos Espa, and a bunch of other
buildings that blend in nicely with the desert.

00:01:50.899 --> 00:01:51.979
Just watch out for all the sand...

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.460
"I don't like sand."

00:01:55.249 --> 00:01:56.560
Gone Girl

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.999
One of the creepier scenes in Gone Girl shows
police visiting an abandoned Missouri mall

00:02:00.999 --> 00:02:03.340
that has been taken over by criminals and
transients.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:07.909
The real mall used in Gone Girl, though, is
actually the former Hawthorne Plaza Mall in

00:02:07.909 --> 00:02:10.119
the Los Angeles suburb of Hawthorne.

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:13.980
Since shutting down in the '90s, the mall
has been used quite a few times in big-budget

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.430
Hollywood movies, including Minority Report
and Evolution.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:18.870
And yes that's how you pronounce it in England.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:19.959
Remember Evolution?

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:22.459
If you don't, please enjoy this representative
sample:

00:02:22.459 --> 00:02:23.459
"Ca-caw?

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:25.380
Ca-caawww.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.140
Ha ha hee hee tooki tooki tooki tooki ca-caw
ca-ca-caw ca-caw!"

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:30.390
The Hunger Games

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:35.099
About a century ago, Henry River Mill Village
in North Carolina was a textile hub.

00:02:35.099 --> 00:02:38.220
But when those jobs started to fade away,
it turned into a ghost town.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:42.710
That made it an ideal shooting location for
the economically depressed District 12 in

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:43.939
The Hunger Games movies.

00:02:43.939 --> 00:02:48.049
Being featured in one of the most popular
film series of all time has injected new interest

00:02:48.049 --> 00:02:50.110
and cash into Henry River Mill Village.

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:54.630
Now, Hunger Games fans can go there and take
a tour of Katniss Everdeen's home town.

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:56.710
Just make sure you get there before it's too
late...

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:58.099
"There is no District 12.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:01.379
It's all gone."

00:03:01.379 --> 00:03:02.689
Iron Man

00:03:02.689 --> 00:03:07.190
In the crucial opening scenes of the first
Marvel Cinematic Universe entry, Tony Stark

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:11.439
is captured in Afghanistan, and imprisoned
in a desert cave by a terrorist cell.

00:03:11.439 --> 00:03:15.349
The real-life cave where this sequence was
shot isn't actually in Afghanistan, though.

00:03:15.349 --> 00:03:20.659
Instead, the crews made their way to an abandoned
mineshaft in Cerro Gordo Mines in California.

00:03:20.659 --> 00:03:23.850
Pretty cool that it wouldn't take much to
visit the set where Marvel Movie History was

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:24.850
born…

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:26.049
"I am Iron Man."

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:28.180
Thanks for watching!

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:31.480
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:34.920
And leave us a comment to let us know which
movie set you'd like to visit...

